---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { BOOKING_URL } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="What is Clinical Myotherapy? | My Myo"
			description="Clinical Myotherapy explained in plain language: what it is, how it differs from physiotherapy, osteopathy and remedial massage, and what a session with James Maddock in Carlton North involves."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>What is Clinical Myotherapy?</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				In plain language: Clinical Myotherapy is the assessment, treatment, and
				management of musculoskeletal pain, by a practitioner trained to work out
				what is actually driving it.
			</p>

			<h2>Where the name comes from</h2>
			<p>
				"Myo" is muscle, so myotherapy is literally "muscle therapy", but the
				"clinical" part matters more. A Clinical Myotherapist holds a Bachelor of
				Health Science (Clinical Myotherapy): a four-year-equivalent degree covering
				anatomy, physiology, orthopaedic and neurological assessment, pain science,
				exercise rehabilitation, and hands-on techniques from joint mobilisation to
				dry needling. Mine was undertaken part-time over six years at Torrens
				University, alongside nearly two decades of clinical practice.
			</p>

			<h2>How it differs from the professions next door</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Remedial massage</strong> is where I started, and it remains the
				foundation of my hands-on work. Think of remedial massage as excellent
				soft-tissue treatment. Clinical Myotherapy keeps those hands-on skills and
				adds a diagnostic layer: structured orthopaedic and neurological testing,
				standardised outcome measures, and a wider treatment toolkit including
				joint mobilisation, dry needling, and exercise prescription.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Physiotherapy</strong> shares the assessment-first ethos; myotherapy
				tends to spend more of the session on manual treatment, with a strong
				emphasis on understanding your pain, not just measuring it.
				<strong>Osteopathy</strong> works from its own philosophical framework of
				whole-body mechanics; my approach is grounded instead in pain neuroscience:
				understanding how the nervous system produces and moderates the pain you
				experience. The honest answer is that all four professions overlap heavily,
				and a good practitioner of any of them will refer you onward when someone
				else can serve you better.
			</p>

			<h2>What a session actually involves</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Assessment first.</strong> History-taking, movement screening,
					and orthopaedic or neurological testing where indicated, so treatment
					targets the driver of your presentation rather than only the sore spot.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Treatment matched to what we find.</strong> That might be soft
					tissue work, joint mobilisation, myofascial release or cupping, dry
					needling, or neurodynamic mobilisation. Techniques are choices, not
					rituals.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Education as treatment.</strong> Pain is data. Understanding
					what your pain means, and what it does not, is often the turning point in
					recovery. You leave knowing what happened and what to do next.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A plan you own.</strong> Self-management and exercise where
					appropriate, paced to your readiness, with your autonomy an explicit
					goal rather than a courtesy.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Who it helps</h2>
			<p>
				Persistent and recurring musculoskeletal pain, headaches, postural and
				overuse conditions, joint stiffness, sporting and workplace injuries,
				surgical recovery, and rebuilding strength and confidence in movement. I
				also have particular experience with hypermobility, perimenopause and
				menopause, neurodivergent clients, and oncology-adjacent support.
			</p>

			<p>
				<a class="cta-button" href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank">Book an appointment</a>
			</p>
			<p>
				Curious about the full toolkit? See <a href="/services">services</a>, or read
				<a href="/about">how and why I practise</a>.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
